<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gestapo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Geheime Staatspolizei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gestapo was the secret state police of Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, created in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a> in 1933 and taken over by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a>, under whose SS it extended across the Reich and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Operating outside judicial control, it hunted political opponents, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and resisters, using informers, torture and detention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>. It took a central part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> and was declared a criminal organisation at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-trials">Nuremberg trials</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gestapo.jpg" alt="Gestapo" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R97512,_Berlin,_Geheimes_Staatspolizeiamt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> became Chancellor, Hermann Goring was named Interior Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, which gave him command of the largest police force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He detached the political and intelligence sections of the police, filled their ranks with Nazis, and on 26 April 1933 merged them as the Geheime Staatspolizei. A post office clerk abbreviated the name to Gestapo for a franking stamp. Goring had wanted to call it the Geheimes Polizeiamt, but its initials looked too like those of the Soviet GPU.</p><p>On 20 April 1934 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a>, head of the SS, took over its oversight, and it became a national agency within the Security Police. From 27 September 1939 it was administered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reich-security-main-office">Reich Security Main Office</a> as its Amt 4.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its first targets were political opponents such as members of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. On 2 May 1933, a day after Hitler addressed 1.5 million people at the Tempelhofer Feld, Gestapo officers who had been shadowing some 58 trade union leaders arrested them, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-labour-front">German Labour Front</a> replaced the unions on 12 May. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-laws">Nuremberg Laws</a> of September 1935 widened the range of denunciable behaviour, including expressions of sympathy toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. The Gestapo also monitored church organisations and arrested the leaders of the White Rose student group in 1943, several of whom were executed. It operated throughout occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where it took part in deporting workers, torturing and executing civilians and murdering Jews, and it held a vital part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Gestapo was smaller than its reputation. By March 1937 it employed an estimated 6,500 people in fifty-four regional offices, and in 1939 only 3,000 of 20,000 men held SS ranks, mostly honorary. Most officers came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>'s police forces. Local offices were thin: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> am Main had 41 men between them in 1939, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-franconia">Lower Franconia</a> twenty-two officers oversaw 840,000 or more inhabitants. It therefore relied on ordinary citizens who denounced others, rather than on full-time informers. Torture was a routine method of extracting confessions.</p><p>The Gestapo was declared a criminal organisation by the International Military Tribunal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, together with the SS. Its chief Heinrich Muller was never tried, having disappeared at the end of the war. In 1997 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> turned the former regional Gestapo headquarters, the EL-DE Haus, into a museum documenting its actions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gestapo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Gestapo</b> grew out of the Prussian political police, which came under Hermann Göring after Hitler named him Reich commissioner of the Interior for Prussia in 1933.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>A German <b>Gestapo</b> report of February 1942 blamed the growth of resistance chiefly on Ustaše atrocities against the Orthodox population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Terror was organised by the SS under Heinrich Himmler, which absorbed the police, ran the <b>Gestapo</b> and the concentration camps from Dachau, opened in March 1933, onwards, and in 1939 combined its security services in the Reich Security Main Office under Reinhard Heydrich.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Vel' d'Hiv Roundup of 16 and 17 July 1942, French police under René Bousquet and Jean Leguay arrested 13,152 Jews, including 4,051 children, whom the <b>Gestapo</b> had not asked for, and sent them through Drancy to Auschwitz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vichy-france">Vichy France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1933 the <b>Gestapo</b> shut it; Mies won permission to reopen, but he and the faculty then agreed to close the school themselves.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Gestapo</b> moved it to the Louvre in June 1944, but when the SS tried to take it on 22 August Paris was again in French hands.</blockquote><a href="/work/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
